Depth-first search, or DFS, follows one branch before trying another. It uses a stack: the newest waiting entry goes next. `pop` removes that entry.
```javascript
const graph = { P: ["Q", "R"], Q: ["S"], R: [], S: ["P"] };
function depthVisits(start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const seen = new Set();
  const order = [];
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (seen.has(node)) {
      continue;
    }
    seen.add(node);
    order.push(node);
    for (const next of [...(graph[node] || [])].reverse()) {
      stack.push(next);
    }
  }
  return order;
}
console.log(depthVisits("P").join(","));
```
Output:
```text
P,Q,S,R
```
`continue` skips a node already seen. Reversing the copied neighbors makes the first listed one come off the stack first. DFS does not generally find the shortest route.
